/* GoT wiki long-form archive — the Watch & beyond the Wall. Original prose; keys match js/characters.js. */
Object.assign(WIKI_EXTRA, {
  "Jeor Mormont": {
    paras: [
      "Jeor Mormont did the thing almost no lord in living memory had done: he gave up a lordship on purpose. Bear Island passed to his son Jorah, and the Old Bear took the black — not in disgrace, not fleeing a headsman, but because he judged the Wall worth the rest of his life. That choice explains everything about how he commands. The Night’s Watch of his tenure is a shell — a few hundred men holding three castles out of nineteen — and Mormont runs it without illusions, husbanding his thieves and poachers like the last coins of a great inheritance.",
      "His grief is a family matter. Jorah shamed the house by selling poachers to slavers and fled the realm rather than face Ned Stark’s sword, and the Old Bear carries that wound the way he carries his raven — constantly, and with a grimace. It is why Jon Snow matters so much to him. In the sulky, talented bastard of Winterfell, Mormont sees a second chance at a son worth the name, and he grooms him with a soldier’s subtlety: naming him steward instead of ranger, keeping him close, teaching him that command is worry with a cloak on.",
      "The gift of Longclaw makes the adoption official. After Jon puts himself between the Lord Commander and a dead man walking — a wight with black hands and blue eyes, loose in the heart of Castle Black — Mormont has the bear’s-head pommel of his house’s ancestral Valyrian blade recut as a wolf and hands five hundred years of Mormont steel to a Stark bastard. He offered the sword to Jorah once. He does not say what it means to give it again; the Old Bear’s tendernesses all arrive disguised as gruffness, usually with the raven muttering “corn” over the top.",
      "His answer to the dark is the Great Ranging — the boldest thing the Watch attempts in the whole tale. Rangers are vanishing, wights are rising, and Mance Rayder is massing every wildling alive; so Mormont marches the strength of the Watch beyond the Wall to learn the truth of it, staging through the grim hospitality of Craster’s Keep to the bare stone summit of the Fist of the First Men. It is the right decision and a ruinous one, which is the Watch’s speciality: the answers he seeks come to the Fist in person, in the cold, with the dead marching in front.",
      "What Mormont understands before anyone else is the scale of the thing. While the realm counts crowns — “these are the wars that matter,” he growls, or words to that effect, at every southern distraction — the Old Bear is counting the dead. His legacy is not a battle won but a message passed: the men he shaped, Jon and Sam above all, carry his war forward after his watch. The Watch that eventually holds the gate against the Long Night is, in every way that counts, the one the Old Bear rebuilt out of scraps.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The tellings agree on the man and diverge on the furniture. The books give his command more texture — the election politics beneath him, the endless quartermastering, a raven that may be rather more than a bird (readers who suspect a skinchanger’s hand in the Watch’s affairs have circumstantial evidence to burn). The show trims the Great Ranging’s scale — the Fist of the First Men falls to the dead on screen in a cold open’s worth of chaos, where the books make it a drawn-out horror recounted in fragments by survivors. His last instructions differ too: on the page his dying thought is Jorah — find my son, tell him to take the black — while the show, having sent Jorah to Daenerys years before, gives his final breath to the Watch and the warning Sam must carry.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "In both tellings the Old Bear survives the Others only to be killed by his own order. The remnant of the Great Ranging staggers back to Craster’s Keep, starving, frostbitten and mutinous, and Craster’s stinginess strikes the spark: the brawl over bread becomes murder, Craster dies at his own board, and when Mormont commands the men to stand down the knives turn on him. On screen it is Rast — the whining bully he’d dragged to the Wall — who drives the blade into his back; Mormont dies throttling him, ordering Sam to reach the Wall with word of what marches behind them.",
      "The books stage the same betrayal with different hands: Dirk’s blade does the work, and the Old Bear lingers long enough for last words that are all about his son — Jorah, the wrong done to the house, the black cloak that would wipe it clean. Either way it is the Watch’s oldest shame enacted in miniature — the order that guards the realms of men murdering the best man in it over supper — and both tellings make sure the mutineers are paid in full before the tale is done.",
    ],
  },
  "Maester Aemon": {
    paras: [
      "The blind old man in the rookery at Castle Black is the tale’s best-kept secret hiding in plain sight: Aemon of House Targaryen, son of King Maekar, brother of King Aegon the Fifth — the boy the family called away to the Citadel because a king’s third son needed an occupation. He forged his chain, and then the succession did what successions do: deaths and abdications marched the crown down the line until it stopped at his feet. Aemon refused it — quietly, absolutely — and took the black so that no faction could ever raise his name against his beloved younger brother Egg. It is possibly the single most consequential renunciation in Westerosi history, and he spent the rest of a century declining to mention it.",
      "By the time the story finds him he has served the Watch for the better part of seventy years, outliving his eyes, his dynasty, and every man who remembered why his name mattered. What remains is the order’s conscience. Recruits know him as the gentle old maester who tends the ravens and the wounded; the attentive notice that Lord Commanders make no great decision without sitting with him first. His counsel runs on one hard-won theme — that the Watch’s vows exist precisely because love is the death of duty, a lesson he delivers to Jon Snow with the unanswerable authority of a man who chose duty three times and paid full price each time.",
      "His bond with Samwell Tarly is the old man’s last apprenticeship. In the fat, frightened steward who reads to him by candlelight, Aemon finds a mind worth furnishing, and Sam finds the first father figure who values him for exactly the qualities Randyll Tarly tried to beat out. It is Aemon’s judgment, exercised at the crucial hour of the Lord Commander election, that helps put Jon Snow at the head of the order — the last great act of kingmaking by the man who refused to be a king.",
      "The news from the east reaches him like sunlight through a closed door. A Targaryen girl across the sea, dragons alive in the world again — and Aemon, a hundred years old and blind, grieves that the last of his blood sits alone among enemies with no kin to counsel her. “A Targaryen alone in the world is a terrible thing,” he says, and the line lands twice: once for Daenerys, and once for the old man saying it, whose entire family has narrowed to a rumor on the wind.",
      "He is the tale’s argument that renunciation can be a form of greatness. Every other Targaryen in the story reaches for the crown or burns in its vicinity; Aemon put it down and lived a century in service instead, and the order he served survives the Long Night substantially because of the men he shaped. The maester’s chain, the black cloak, the empty sockets that see further than most men’s eyes — he wears a life’s worth of surrendered things, and carries them more lightly than any king in the story carries a crown.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show keeps Aemon at Castle Black to the end; the books send him on one last journey. When Melisandre arrives hunting king’s blood, Sam smuggles the old man south by sea to keep him out of her fires — through Braavos, where the news of Daenerys reaches him, and out onto the long water aboard the Cinnamon Wind. Dying, he burns bright: it is book-Aemon who works out that the prophecy of the prince that was promised may point at Daenerys, that the dragon must have three heads, and who charges Sam to reach the Citadel and send help to her. The show, having no Citadel voyage to give him, folds his farewells into Castle Black and hands his prophetic function to other mouths. Both tellings agree on the essentials: the refused crown, the blindness, Egg, and a death gentler than any other Targaryen is granted.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "On screen he dies as he lived, in his bed at Castle Black, ancient past reckoning, laughing softly with people sixty years dead — “Egg, I dreamed that I was old” — while Sam and Gilly keep the vigil. The Watch burns him with full honors, and even Alliser Thorne’s eulogy is gentle: a hundred years of service ended without an enemy in the world.",
      "The books give him a sailor’s death instead. Carried south to escape the red woman, he weakens past Braavos, and at one hundred and two years old dies aboard the Cinnamon Wind, tended by Sam and Gilly, dreaming of his brother and murmuring about dragons — the last work of his life, the riddle of the three heads, spilling out with his final breaths. The crew commits him to the sea. In both tellings the same words close the account, and nowhere in the saga do they land harder: and now his watch is ended.",
    ],
  },
  "Alliser Thorne": {
    paras: [
      "Ser Alliser Thorne is what loyalty looks like after history has finished with it. A knight of the Crownlands, he fought for the Targaryens to the bitter end of Robert’s Rebellion, and when the dust settled the victors offered him the standard mercy of the age: the Wall or worse. So the Watch inherited a proud, capable, permanently embittered man — and made him master-at-arms, with a license to grind every new recruit under the heel of his disappointment.",
      "His training-yard method is cruelty as curriculum. He hangs mocking names on the boys — Lord Snow for the bastard with the polished sword-work, Ser Piggy for the fat one who cannot hold a blade — and sets them against each other to find out who breaks. The defensible version of his logic is that the Wall kills soft men and it is better they break in the yard; the truth underneath is plainer, that a man cheated of his cause enjoys having someone to punish. Jon Snow, wellborn, gifted and adored by the other recruits, offends him on every axis at once, and the enmity between them becomes one of the Watch’s load-bearing structures.",
      "The wildling assault is his finest hour, and it is genuinely fine. With the Lord Commander dead beyond the Wall and Mance Rayder’s host at the gate, Thorne holds Castle Black — commanding from the top of the Wall, then taking the fight hand-to-hand in the yard against Tormund Giantsbane, roaring at his brothers that they need to fight and being entirely correct. Whatever else the man is, he is no coward, and the defense of the Wall is the one night his hardness and the Watch’s need point the same direction.",
      "Then Jon Snow beats him for the Lord Commandership, and everything curdles. Thorne serves — made First Ranger, obeying orders he despises — while Jon opens the gates to the free folk Thorne has spent a career fighting. To Thorne this is not policy but treason against the dead: every brother killed by wildling raiders, unavenged and now unremembered, in the name of a threat he half-believes in at best. He tells Jon to his face that he has a good heart and that it will get them all killed. It is the tale’s tidy trick that the Watch’s most honest warning and its greatest crime come from the same mouth.",
      "Thorne matters because he is the Watch’s institutional grudge made flesh — the argument that an order built to guard men cannot survive being asked to love them. Set against Jon’s mercy, he loses the argument and the future; but the story declines to make him small. He states his reasons in daylight, signs his name to his deed, and never once asks anyone’s pardon, which is more than most of the tale’s betrayers manage.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Book-Thorne is a pettier creature with a smaller part. He duels Jon with insults rather than conspiracies, and his great humiliation is an embassy: sent to King’s Landing with a wight’s severed hand to prove the dead are rising, he waits so long for an audience that the hand rots to bone, and Tyrion laughs the Watch’s evidence out of court — a scene that quietly explains why the realm never came to help. By the time of Jon’s command, the books have shipped him off to Eastwatch and then out on a ranging from which he has not returned; he has no part in the mutiny, whose knives belong to Bowen Marsh and the stewards. The show fused Thorne with that conspiracy to give Jon’s murder a familiar face — dramatically efficient, and a real change: the books’ betrayal comes from the Watch’s cautious middle, not its old grudge.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "In the show he finishes what he started. Leading the mutineers, he puts a knife in Jon Snow’s heart beside the sign that reads traitor, holds Castle Black against Davos and the loyalists, and loses when the free folk arrive with a giant. When Jon returns from the dead, his first and last act as Lord Commander is the sentence: Thorne hangs beside Olly and the other mutineers, and goes to the rope with his spine straight — he fought, he lost, now he rests, and he’d do it all again. Jon cuts the ropes down himself and leaves the Watch with the bodies.",
      "In the published books he is alive, so far as anyone knows — dispatched beyond the Wall on a ranging by the Lord Commander he loathed, and not yet returned when the knives came out at Castle Black. Whether Jon meant the mission as employment or exile is left for the reader to weigh; Thorne himself suspected the latter, loudly.",
    ],
  },
  "Yoren": {
    paras: [
      "Yoren is the Night’s Watch as the realm actually meets it: not a silver knight on a wall of ice but a crook-backed, sour-smelling man in faded blacks, working the kingsroad and the dungeons, chewing sourleaf until his teeth run red and asking magistrates for their condemned. A wandering crow, the Watch calls the job — thirty years of walking the Seven Kingdoms collecting rapers, poachers and orphan boys, because the order that guards the realms of men must be built out of whatever the realm throws away.",
      "He is in King’s Landing on Watch business when the world breaks, and it makes him briefly the most important man in the story. In the crowd at the Great Sept of Baelor, as Ned Stark’s confession turns to butchery, it is Yoren who spots the small girl on the statue of Baelor — and moves. He hauls Arya Stark off her plinth before she can see the sword fall, shears her hair in an alley, names her Arry the orphan boy, and folds the most hunted child in Westeros into his recruit column, because he owed Ned Stark and because it was right, in roughly that order.",
      "The march north shows what the man is made of. His column is a walking argument for despair — orphans, gutter-thieves, and three caged horrors he will not speak of — and he shepherds it through a riverlands sliding into war with curses, cuffs and an entirely unsentimental competence. His counsel to Arya, delivered rough, is the sort that keeps people alive; his own story, told by a night fire — the brother killed, the axe he buried in the killer’s skull, the Wall that took him after — is the Watch’s whole recruitment model in a single grim anecdote.",
      "He never gets them there, and the manner of his failing is the point. When armed men in the king’s colors demand his recruits, Yoren — a black brother, sworn to no side, owed passage by every law and custom in Westeros — refuses on principle, and discovers that the war has already eaten the customs. The Night’s Watch takes no part: he says it, believes it, and the story uses him to show it is no longer true. Every article of the old order’s protection fails on that road, and the children he was guarding walk into the war’s machinery without him.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The two tellings walk the same road with different scenery. In the books the ambush comes at an abandoned holdfast by the Gods Eye, where Yoren’s courtesy is answered by Ser Amory Lorch’s assault; the show moves the fight to a burning roadside camp and gives the demand a different pretext — the gold cloaks hunting Gendry, Robert’s bastard, whom Yoren refuses to yield with a knife at a throat and a speech about fat men and sharp steel. Book-Yoren’s cargo is also heavier: it is he who carts Rorge, Biter and the courteous monster called Jaqen H’ghar north in chains, a decision whose consequences ripple through Arya’s whole saga. Both versions agree on the essentials — the sourleaf, the shears, the debt to Ned Stark paid in full.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He dies as he lived, refusing to hand anyone over. In the books, Lorch’s soldiers storm the holdfast by night and Yoren goes down fighting in the gate, an axe in him, still swinging, buying his recruits the minutes some of them use to live. The show gives him the same death by another road: surrounded at the camp, he declines the gold cloaks’ warrant with open contempt, takes several of Lorch’s men with him, and is finally pinned and finished — a crossbow bolt and a blade ending thirty years of service in a ditch on the kingsroad. In both tellings the promise survives him: Arya Stark walks out of the wreckage carrying the lessons and the fury, and the men who killed him start appearing on a certain list.",
    ],
  },
  "Grenn": {
    paras: [
      "Grenn arrives at the Wall the way most of its men do: unwanted. A farmer’s son from the Reach, abandoned or orphaned into the black, he is big, slow of speech and easy to mock — Ser Alliser dubs him Aurochs for his bulk and his wits, and the name is meant to draw blood. What the yard discovers, slowly, is that the joke has a floor: Grenn absorbs mockery without cruelty, learns without vanity, and is entirely without malice in an institution assembled largely from malice.",
      "He is one of the first stones in Jon Snow’s new life. After Jon humiliates the other recruits in the yard, it is Grenn and Pyp he teaches instead of taunts, and the little brotherhood that forms — Jon, Grenn, Pyp, Sam — becomes the Watch’s honest heart. Grenn’s decency has weight because it costs him: he stands up for Sam against Thorne’s bullying when standing up has a price, and he swears his vows before the old gods beside Jon in the haunted forest, a farm boy adopting a faith because his friend keeps it.",
      "The Great Ranging makes a veteran of him. He digs at the Fist of the First Men, survives the dead when they come, and staggers back through the snows with the remnant — one of the few who witness the whole shape of the Watch’s true war and live to stand a wall afterward. By the night Mance’s host reaches Castle Black, the aurochs of the training yard has become something the Watch is always short of: a brother who has seen the worst and holds his ground anyway.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Book-Grenn survives the series as published — through the Fist, the mutiny’s aftermath and the defense of Castle Black, still cheerfully enduring the old nickname, last seen going about the Watch’s business. His show death at the inner gate is an invention, and a pointed one: the screen version gathers the story’s affection for the Watch’s ordinary men into one stand and spends it all at once. Readers should also note the book’s fuller comedy of his friendship with Pyp — the pair function as a double act through three novels — where the show, with less time, sketches it in glances.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "The show gives him the death of a much grander name. When Mance’s giant, Mag the Mighty, forces the tunnel under the Wall, Grenn and five brothers are sent down to hold the inner gate — six men against a king of the giants in a corridor of ice. Jon finds them afterward: the gate held, the giant dead, and all six brothers fallen where they stood. Grenn led them in the vow as they died — the shield that guards the realms of men — and the tale offers no cleaner statement of what the Night’s Watch is for. He is alive in the published books.",
    ],
  },
  "Pyp": {
    paras: [
      "Pypar — Pyp to everyone — comes to the Wall from a mummers’ troupe, which makes him that rarest of recruits: one whose crime was a talent. The story he tells is that a lord’s son took offense; the story he performs is everything else, for Pyp can do every accent in the Seven Kingdoms and most of the officers’ voices, and a cell of frightened recruits needs a jester more than it needs another sword.",
      "He is the quick half of the Watch’s gentlest friendship, paired with big, patient Grenn in an endless double act of insults and loyalty, and one of the first to close ranks around Jon Snow and then around Sam. It is Pyp’s ear for men — a mummer reads a room the way a maester reads a book — that makes him useful beyond the japes: he knows which brothers can be trusted, which are dangerous, and precisely how far Ser Alliser can be pushed, information of real value in a fortress full of knives.",
      "When the war comes to Castle Black he stands his watch with the rest, a slight man with a bow on a wall built for giants. The Watch has no use for what Pyp was and every use for what he chooses to be, which is the order’s whole wager on its broken men stated small: the mummer’s boy holds the Wall.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "In the books Pyp survives — he fights through the siege of Castle Black and is alive as the published story leaves the Wall, grumbling in company with Grenn. The show kills him at the gate, an arrow in the throat during the wildling assault, largely to give the battle a cost the audience feels by name. The books also give his election-night self more to do: Pyp and Grenn are part of the whispering campaign that helps Sam maneuver Jon into the Lord Commandership, the mummer’s gifts turned at last to politics.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "His show death comes in the battle for Castle Black, and the arrow is Ygritte’s. Manning the wall of the yard, Pyp takes the shaft through the throat and dies in Samwell Tarly’s arms, frightened and quick and far too young, while the fight roars on without pausing for him — the battle’s first hard lesson that this night will be paid for by name. He is alive in the published books.",
    ],
  },
  "Edd Tollett": {
    paras: [
      "Eddison Tollett — Dolorous Edd, and never was a name better earned — is a poor relation of a minor Vale house who brought to the Night’s Watch the one weapon it never runs short of use for: pessimism of the very highest quality. Every duty is a doom, every meal a disappointment, every morning a personal affront, and he narrates all of it in a flat, funereal deadpan that becomes the Wall’s unofficial anthem. The joke, made carefully and at length across the whole tale, is that the Watch’s gloomiest man is among its most reliable.",
      "He serves as squire and steward through the worst of it — the Great Ranging, the horror on the Fist, the mutiny at Craster’s — grumbling steadily through events that break stronger-seeming men. That is the design of the character: Edd’s complaints are a coping mechanism wearing a black cloak, and the men around him lean on it. When Jon Snow rises, Edd rises alongside as his steward and then his friend, one of the few brothers whose loyalty never wavers and never needs stating.",
      "His finest hours are rescues. It is Edd who rides for the free folk when Jon needs the Wall’s enemies saved from a worse one, Edd who holds Castle Black steady in the ugly days after the mutiny against Jon, and Edd who accepts — with visible dismay, as though handed a chamber pot — the Lord Commandership itself when Jon lays down his cloak. The Watch’s last commander of the old dispensation is a man who never wanted anything, which the story quietly suggests is the only safe qualification for power.",
      "Edd’s meaning is proportion. In a saga of kings and prophecies he represents the enlisted man’s-eye view: the cold is cold, the food is bad, the officers are mad, and the work gets done anyway. His humor is the sound of endurance, and the Watch he ends up leading — smaller, stranger, full of wildlings he once fought — survives partly because its commander long ago stopped expecting anything of the world except the worst, and showing up regardless.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Book-Edd is alive and complaining as the published story stands — Jon posts him to command at Long Barrow, garrisoned with spearwives, a billet Edd accepts with predictable despair — and he has no death to his name on the page. The show promoted him further than the books yet have, to acting Lord Commander after Jon’s departure, and then spent him in the Long Night. His humor translates almost untouched between tellings; several of the screen’s best Edd lines are book lines with the serial numbers rubbed off, which is its own kind of fidelity.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He dies in the Long Night at Winterfell doing precisely the thing he would have grumbled hardest about: saving Samwell Tarly. In the crush of the dead inside the walls, Edd hauls Sam up from under a pile of wights, turns to cover him — and takes a blade through the back, dying on his feet with his usual expression of weary vindication barely disturbed. He rises with the dead before the night is out and is put down with the rest when the Night King falls, then laid on the pyres with the honored dead of the living. He is alive in the published books.",
    ],
  },
  "Janos Slynt": {
    paras: [
      "Janos Slynt is the tale’s field study in the small man promoted. A butcher’s son who climbed the City Watch of King’s Landing to its command, he learned the capital’s true civics early: gold cloaks answer to gold, and virtue is a rumor spread by people who can afford it. Under Robert he sells the Watch’s honesty piecemeal — commands bought, brothels shaken down — and survives every audit because the men who might sack him find him too usefully for sale.",
      "His hour arrives with Ned Stark’s. Promised the gold cloaks’ support, the Hand of the King moves against Joffrey’s succession — and Slynt, purchased in advance by Littlefinger, turns the City Watch on Stark’s men in the throne room. The reward tells the whole story of the new regime: the butcher’s boy is made Lord of Harrenhal, granted a seat on the small council, and founds his noble line on the neat, bloody work of betraying the most honorable man in the kingdom. It is Slynt’s men, the story takes care to note, who carry out the massacre of Robert’s bastard children — infants included — without recorded objection.",
      "Tyrion Lannister, arriving as Hand, does the arithmetic in one dinner: a man who sold the last Hand will sell the next one. He ships Slynt to the Wall — dressed as an honor, executed as a purge — and the capital’s judgment becomes the Watch’s problem. At Castle Black, Slynt does what he has always done: seeks the strong (Ser Alliser), bullies the weak, and campaigns for the Lord Commandership as though the Wall were one more city command to be bought. The Watch, inconveniently for him, holds elections it cannot rig, and the bastard boy he sneered at wins it.",
      "Slynt’s function in the story is to test what institutions do with rot. King’s Landing promoted him twice; the Wall is the first place that ever told him no. His refusal to accept that answer — banking on friends at court, certain the old rules of purchase still apply somewhere — collides with the one commander in Westeros who watched his father die of exactly those rules. The collision is brief, public, and much cited afterward.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The tellings differ mainly in choreography and company. In the books Slynt arrives at the Wall with his cronies in tow and comes within a few votes of winning the Lord Commandership — a genuinely near thing, averted by Sam’s midnight politicking — which makes his subsequent insubordination a sore loser’s gamble rather than mere spite. The order he refuses differs too: on the page it is command of the ruined castle of Greyguard; on screen, oversight of the same sort of restoration work. Both versions agree that he refuses three times, publicly, banking on Lannister protection that no longer exists, and that the sentence that follows is carried out by the Lord Commander’s own hand — the show adding a last-second collapse into begging, the books adding Stannis Baratheon’s silent, approving nod from the King’s Tower.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "Ordered to take charge of a derelict castle down the Wall, Slynt refuses — once with scorn, twice with cronies at his back, a third time with open contempt for the boy commander. Jon Snow does not hang him. He orders the block brought out into the yard of Castle Black and takes Janos Slynt’s head himself with Longclaw, in the old northern way his father taught: the man who passes the sentence swings the sword. In the show Slynt’s bluster shatters at the last into terror — “I’ve always been afraid” — and the stroke falls anyway; in the books his end is steadier but no less final, and Stannis, watching, nods once. Either way the debt sworn on the steps of Baelor’s sept years before is paid in full, by the son of the man he sold.",
    ],
  },
  "Qhorin Halfhand": {
    paras: [
      "Qhorin gets his name from an axe: a wildling blade took all the fingers of his sword hand but thumb and forefinger, and he taught himself to fight with his left, and became more dangerous than before. That is the whole man in one anecdote. The senior ranger of the Shadow Tower is the Watch’s living legend — the free folk frighten children with him — and he carries the reputation the way he carries everything, quietly, as equipment.",
      "The Great Ranging brings him together with Jon Snow, and the pairing is deliberate: when scouting parties are told off into the Skirling Pass, the Halfhand asks for the green boy with the direwolf. What follows is the shortest and most consequential mentorship in the tale. In days, Qhorin teaches Jon the ranger’s creed — the Watch is the sword in the darkness, and the man is nothing beside the mission — not as a speech but as a way of moving through hostile country, eating cold, sleeping light, and spending nothing you cannot afford.",
      "When the wildlings close the pass and the hunted party dwindles, Qhorin makes the calculation his whole life has been practice for. The Watch needs a man inside Mance Rayder’s host more than it needs another dead ranger; wildlings trust only turncloaks who have proven the turn. So he gives Jon the hardest order in the story — break your vows, join them, do whatever they ask of you — and then arranges the proof himself, in the full knowledge of what the proof must be.",
      "Everything Jon Snow later becomes runs through the Halfhand’s arithmetic. The spy Qhorin planted comes back knowing the free folk as people rather than raiders, and that knowledge — bought with Qhorin’s life and Jon’s honor — reshapes the Watch’s whole war, opens the gates to the wildlings, and ultimately stocks the lands south of the Wall with the living rather than the dead. The Watch asks everything of its men; Qhorin is the man who gave it gladly, and the tale keeps his ledger open long after his ranging ends.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The screen compresses the Skirling Pass — the long hunt through the heights, the dwindling of the scouting party man by man, and Qhorin’s methodical grooming of Jon for the role all shrink to a few scenes of captivity, with the Halfhand sowing Jon’s cover story from inside the wildling column by picking loud fights with him. The books keep the two free until the end and give the final fight a witness the show omits: Ghost, whose teeth close on Qhorin’s calf in the last exchange, letting the ranger die knowing the tale the wildlings will tell is airtight. His great line — “our honor means no more than our lives, so long as the realm is safe” — is the books’ cleanest statement of the Watch’s creed, and the show wisely keeps its substance.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He dies on Jon Snow’s sword, exactly as he planned. In the books, cornered in the Skirling Pass with the Rattleshirt’s hunters closing, Qhorin names Jon his brother one last time, then comes at him like a storm — giving the boy no choice and the watching wildlings no doubt — and falls to Longclaw with Ghost at his leg, murmuring that sharp steel is a better death than the ones on offer. The show stages the same transaction inside the wildling camp: a picked quarrel, a seized blade, a fight Qhorin conducts like a lesson until Jon runs him through before Rattleshirt’s whole band. His last words bless the mission — and the free folk, believing they have watched a crow kill a crow, open their world to the spy the Halfhand died to plant. The Watch never gives him a pyre; his monument is everything Jon does after.",
    ],
  },
  "Will of the Night's Watch": {
    paras: [
      "The entire saga opens through the eyes of a poacher. Will came to the Wall the usual way — caught red-handed skinning a buck in lands belonging to House Mallister, offered the choice between a hand and a cloak — and four years of ranging turned the woodsman’s stealth that damned him into the Watch’s asset. He can move through the haunted forest like smoke, and it is exactly that skill that puts him where the story begins: on point, in the trees, watching a camp of dead wildlings that will not stay dead.",
      "Will is the tale’s first witness. He sees what the reader sees — the cold deepening, the shadows that move against the wind, the pale swords — and he understands it immediately, because unlike his lordly commander he has never had the luxury of doubting the dark. His warning goes unheeded; his prayer, made in a tree with a sword he has forgotten how to hold, goes unanswered. Everything the story will spend years teaching kings and councils, it teaches a poacher in its first pages, and then demonstrates the price of the lesson.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The book and the show quietly swap the fates of the ranging’s two survivors. On the page, Will climbs a sentinel tree when the Others take Ser Waymar apart, and what comes up the trunk after him wears his commander’s face; it is Gared who flees south to be executed as a deserter. The show folds the two men together and hands the desertion, the flight, and Ned Stark’s sword to Will — making him the wide-eyed deserter whose garbled warning about white shadows opens the series proper and whose death introduces the Stark creed of the man who passes the sentence. Same prologue, same lesson; the tellings simply disagree about which broken man carried it to Winterfell.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "In the books Will dies in the haunted forest, minutes after the story begins. Hidden aloft, he watches the pale shadows shatter Waymar Royce’s sword and leave the boy dead in the snow — and then the corpse rises, and climbs. The last thing Will feels is cold fingers at his throat: killed by the wight of the commander he could not save, the saga’s first named death and its thesis statement in one.",
      "In the show he lives a little longer and dies warmer. Fleeing the massacre, he makes it south of the Wall — the first deserter of the story — and is taken near Winterfell, where he tells Lord Eddard Stark, calmly and without hope, exactly what he saw. Ned hears him out, calls him deserter, and swings the greatsword himself while the Stark children watch. The realm’s first warning of the White Walkers dies as a criminal, believed by no one; the audience, who saw what he saw, spends the rest of the tale knowing he told the truth.",
    ],
  },
  "Ser Waymar Royce": {
    paras: [
      "Waymar Royce is what happens when the great houses tithe their surplus sons to the Wall. Third son of Bronze Yohn Royce of Runestone, he arrives with a warhorse, a sable-trimmed cloak and the total confidence of a boy who has never been contradicted by anything larger than a sparring partner — and the Watch, chronically starved of highborn officers, gives him a command at eighteen. Veterans with forty years of rangings ride at the orders of a knight half a year off his father’s practice yard.",
      "The tale is scrupulously fair to him, which is what makes the prologue sting. Waymar is arrogant, yes — he mocks his men’s fears, overrules the woodsman’s instincts of better rangers, and pushes the patrol on through a cold that older hands know is wrong — but when the impossible steps out of the trees, the boy lordling does not run. He draws. Alone, outnumbered by things out of a nurse’s tale, he levels his sword at an Other and calls for the dance, and for a few strokes the last knight of the age of heroes is a spoiled third son from the Vale.",
      "His function in the story is to be the reader’s first casualty of the gap between the world men believe in and the world that is. Every institution that made Waymar — house, knighthood, Watch — equipped him for the wrong war. His courage is real and it purchases nothing, which is the coldest sentence the prologue passes; the tale will spend eighty-odd chapters before anyone south of that clearing takes the lesson seriously.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The prologue’s duel belongs to the books: the mocking, patient Other, the crystal sword that shivers Waymar’s blade to splinters, the watchers who emerge from the wood to finish the work with cold courtesy. The show shortens the encounter to a massacre glimpsed in the dark — Waymar dies quickly and mostly off-camera, and the eerie fencing-lesson quality of the book scene is spent elsewhere. Readers may also enjoy the long echo the books give him: seasons later, his father Bronze Yohn passes through the story still ignorant of how his son died, and the Vale plotline keeps House Royce’s grief quietly in view. The show’s Royces appear too, but the connection to the frozen boy from the first scene is never drawn.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He dies in the haunted forest on his first ranging, the first sworn brother lost in the saga. In the books it is nearly a formal duel: an Other steps from the dark, blades meet, and the inhuman thing toys with him — parry by parry, in a cold that makes steel scream — until Waymar’s sword bursts and the shard takes his eye, and then the watching Others close in together and cut the boy apart. What rises afterward wears his sables and his wounds and strangles the last of his men in a tree. The show gives him the same end with less ceremony, cut down in the dark by the first White Walker the audience ever sees. Either way his corpse is the story’s first proof that beyond the Wall, dying is not the end of one’s service.",
    ],
  },
  "Gared": {
    paras: [
      "Gared has spent forty of his fifty-odd years in the Night’s Watch — a lifetime of rangings that cost him both ears and three toes to frostbite and taught him the only lore that matters north of the Wall: respect the cold. He is the veteran of the doomed patrol that opens the tale, the man who argues for turning back, who names the wrongness in the air while his boy commander laughs, and who is proven right in the worst way an old soldier can be.",
      "His part is small and structural: Gared is the institutional memory the Watch no longer listens to. Set beside Ser Waymar’s sable cloak, his cropped ears and careful fear are the order’s two eras in one frame — the Watch that remembers what it was built against, taking orders from the Watch that thinks the enemy is wildlings. When the pale shadows come, the man who survives them is the one who believed in them, and his reward for surviving is the block.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The tellings trade his ending with Will’s. In the books it is Gared who escapes the massacre, deserts, and is run down in the lands near Winterfell; the second chapter opens with Ned Stark taking his head at a holdfast while Bran watches and learns the old way. The show gives that scene to Will and keeps Gared beyond the Wall, where the White Walkers behead him and set his head rolling at Will’s feet as a message. Book-Gared also gets a past the show omits — Old Nan’s tales aside, he has ridden with the Old Bear himself, and his terror lands harder for coming from a man with nothing left to prove.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "In the published books Gared flees the horror in the haunted forest and keeps going — past the Wall, past his vows, half-mad with what he has seen — until Stark men take him near Winterfell as a deserter. Lord Eddard hears no useful sense from him, passes the sentence, and swings Ice himself; the story’s first execution, conducted by its most honorable man, kills the realm’s only living witness to its true enemy. The show ends him earlier and colder: beheaded in the snow by a White Walker’s blade, his head tossed contemptuously at the feet of the comrade who will carry the warning south in his place.",
    ],
  },
  "Chett": {
    paras: [
      "Chett is the Night’s Watch with the romance scraped off. A leechman’s son from the fetid fringes of the riverlands, sent to the Wall for a killing over a girl who laughed at him, he found in the order the one comfort it reliably offers the graceless: a soft berth, tending Maester Aemon’s ravens and leeches in the warmth of the rookery. Then Samwell Tarly arrived — fat, wellborn, kindly — and was handed Chett’s post, and Chett was sent to the kennels, and hatred found the vocation it had been waiting for all his life.",
      "The prologue of the third book lives inside that hatred, on the freezing summit of the Fist of the First Men. Chett has done his sums: the Great Ranging is doomed, the officers are fools, and he has quietly assembled more than a dozen like-minded brothers into a plan — kill the Old Bear and the officers in the night, scatter, and live soft off a wildling village he knows. His inventory of grudges, chief among them the boy who took his leeches and the fat lordling who took his warm fire, is the Watch’s recruitment ledger read from the bottom: not every man the realm throws away was thrown away unjustly.",
      "He matters because he shows the mutiny before the mutiny. The knives that eventually turn at Craster’s Keep do not come from nowhere; Chett’s conspiracy on the Fist is the same rot surveyed a book early, interrupted rather than cured. The tale gives its most sour man its most bitterly ironic vantage — he is standing his hated watch, nursing his plan, when the horn blows once, then twice, and then a third time, and every grievance in his ledger is abruptly beside the point.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Chett never appears in the show at all; his function is divided among the mutineers the screen does name. Rast inherits his whining malice, Karl Tanner his predatory leadership, and the general grievance of the Watch’s worst men — that they were promised survival, not sacrifice — arrives on screen fully formed at Craster’s Keep without the slow fuse the book prologue lights. Show-Sam also keeps a trace of him: the hostility Sam meets from certain brothers over his soft duties is Chett’s grudge with the name filed off. Readers meet him only once, in the third book’s prologue, but his eyes are the ones through which the books stage the horn blasts that announce the Others’ assault on the Fist — one of the saga’s great curtain-raisings.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "His end comes on the Fist of the First Men, the night his mutiny was to begin. The plan is set, the knives are picked — and then the horn sounds three times, the signal no living brother has ever heard, and the Others fall on the Great Ranging out of the storm. The prologue closes with Chett fumbling frozen-fingered at his blade as the snow walks toward him, all his careful spite converted in an instant into the terror of a small man in a very old war; the books leave him among the hundreds who never came down from the Fist. The show, having no Chett, owed him no death — his mutiny simply detonates later, at Craster’s, in other men’s hands.",
    ],
  },
  "Samwell Tarly": {
    paras: [
      "Samwell Tarly’s story begins with a father’s ultimatum. The eldest son of Horn Hill — heir to Heartsbane and to Lord Randyll’s iron reputation — was soft, bookish, frightened of blood and incurably gentle, and Randyll Tarly tried everything short of murder to correct it before settling on murder’s formal cousin: take the black and renounce your inheritance, or suffer a hunting accident. So Sam arrives at Castle Black self-described — a craven, he says, before anyone else can — carrying the precise inventory of qualities the Wall despises and, as it turns out, the ones it most desperately lacks.",
      "Jon Snow’s protection keeps him alive through Thorne’s yard; Maester Aemon’s rookery gives him a use. Named steward to the ancient maester, Sam discovers the Watch’s true armory — its library — and becomes the order’s memory at exactly the moment its old war returns. It is Sam who digs the dragonglass cache out of the snows at the Fist, Sam who finds in crumbling annals what the Long Night was and what stops the Others, Sam who understands that the Watch’s enemy cannot be fought until it is first believed in and read about.",
      "The Great Ranging makes him a legend against every expectation including his own. In the shattering retreat from the Fist, with a White Walker striding through the snow to take Gilly’s newborn son, the craven of Horn Hill drives a dragonglass dagger into the thing and watches it come apart into steaming ice — the first man in thousands of years to kill an Other. The brothers dub him Sam the Slayer, half in mockery; the mockery ages badly. He follows it by doing something harder still, smuggling Gilly and her son south from Craster’s Keep through a forest full of the dead, one terrified step at a time.",
      "His other great weapon is gentler: Sam is the tale’s most effective backstage politician. It is Sam who engineers Jon Snow’s election as Lord Commander, trading whispers and counting votes while stronger men bluster; Sam who argues the free folk’s case from the annals; Sam who keeps Gilly and little Sam alive through every relocation the war demands. Love, in his hands, is a discipline. The boy who could not hold a sword builds a family out of a wildling girl and another man’s baby and defends it with more constancy than most knights defend their vows.",
      "The Citadel ought to be his triumph and is instead his education in institutions. Sent to Oldtown to forge a maester’s chain for the Watch, he finds the keepers of the realm’s knowledge as uninterested in the apocalypse as its kings — and responds, in the show’s telling, in character: he steals the restricted books, cures Ser Jorah’s greyscale because the manual said it was possible and no one else would try, and walks out of the Citadel with the learning and without the permission. Along the way he mines from the archives the record that remakes the story — the annulment and secret marriage that mean Jon Snow is the trueborn heir to the Iron Throne — and delivers the realm’s biggest secret with the realm’s smallest fanfare.",
      "By the end the show has seated him on the small council as Grand Maester, proposing democracy to a room that laughs him down and settling for writing the history instead. It is the right ending for the character the whole tale has been arguing for: the notion that courage counts the same in a library as on a wall, and that the fat boy his father was ashamed of would do more to save the realms of men than the whole roll of the Watch’s swordsmen. His father never understood him; the story makes sure everyone else does.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The books leave Sam earlier on the same road, and give the road more weight. His voyage to Oldtown is an odyssey — Braavos, storms, grief — undertaken with Gilly, Aemon, and a secret cargo of sorrow: Jon has swapped Gilly’s baby for Mance Rayder’s to keep the king-beyond-the-Wall’s son from Melisandre’s fires, and Gilly’s grief shadows every league. Aemon dies at sea deciphering prophecy; Sam arrives at the Citadel to find a novice who is not what he seems and a war for knowledge already quietly underway. None of the show’s later inventions — Heartsbane taken from Horn Hill, the greyscale cure, the parentage revelation, the Grand Maester’s chain — exist yet on the page.",
      "The tellings also weigh his family differently. The show stages the Horn Hill dinner and gives Randyll Tarly a face and a firing squad of insults, then has Daenerys burn father and brother alike — handing Sam a grief the books have not dealt him. Book-Randyll remains a distant, dreaded fact rather than a scene partner. And the screen’s Sam is braver sooner; the books keep his terror closer to the surface for longer, which makes each act of courage — the Other, the wights at the weirwood grove, the election scheming — cost visibly more.",
    ],
  },
  "Gilly": {
    paras: [
      "Gilly is born into the smallest and worst world in the story: Craster’s Keep, where the daughters become wives and the sons are given to the cold. Named for the gillyflower, one of nineteen-odd women serving a father-husband who owns everything in sight including them, she has never seen a map, a coin, or a kindness with no price on it. What she has, when the Night’s Watch first shelters at the keep, is the one thing her world cannot afford: a child coming, and the certainty of what Craster does with boys.",
      "Her first act in the tale is its bravest per ounce: she asks for help. Approaching a sworn brother — fat, frightened Sam, the least alarming crow in the yard — to beg passage south for herself and her unborn child is a treason against everything she has ever been taught, punishable by the god in the yard and the husband at the hearth. The first asking fails. The world has to fall apart — the mutiny, Craster dead, the keep in flames — before Sam can finally take her hand, and the flight that follows, a craven and a wildling girl carrying a newborn through a forest full of the dead, is one of the tale’s quiet epics.",
      "South of the Wall, Gilly’s story becomes an education conducted at speed. Castle Black, Mole’s Town, ships, cities, castles — every stop teaches her something about the enormous world and teaches the audience something about her: that she is quick, observant, unsentimental, and far better at survival than the institutions sheltering her. She learns her letters and takes to them; it is Gilly, reading aloud from a High Septon’s dusty diary, who first turns up the record of Rhaegar’s annulment — the hinge of the entire saga’s secret — while Sam, buried in worthier volumes, nearly talks over her.",
      "Her bond with Sam is the story’s least complicated love and one of its most argued-for. He is the first man who ever protected her without owning her; she is the first person who ever looked at Sam and saw neither a disappointment nor a joke. The family they assemble — her son called little Sam, raised by a man with no obligation to him — is built in direct defiance of both their fathers’ houses: Craster’s keep, where children were currency, and Horn Hill, where they were assets. Between them they raise the first child in the tale to be merely loved.",
      "By the end she has crossed the entire map, outlived the world that made her, and stands at Sam’s side expecting another child — a woman who began the story as property in a nameless keep and ends it as the wife of the realm’s Grand Maester in all but title. The tale is stocked with transformations bought by swords and dragons; Gilly’s is bought by asking for help, learning to read, and refusing at every step to pass on the cruelty she was raised in. It may be the least resounding heroism in the story, and the most complete.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The great divergence is the baby swap. In the books, Jon Snow — fearing Melisandre will burn any child of king’s blood — secretly exchanges Gilly’s son for Mance Rayder’s newborn and sends Gilly south nursing the wrong child, an act of cold arithmetic that leaves her grieving down the whole length of the voyage to Oldtown; her ship-board sorrow, and Sam’s guilt, color everything the books do with them. The show cuts the swap entirely, and with it that grief: screen-Gilly keeps her own boy, and her journey with Sam runs through invented stations — Mole’s Town under wildling attack, the frosty dinner table at Horn Hill, the Citadel’s scullery — toward a settled domesticity the books have not yet granted. Her diary discovery about Rhaegar’s marriage is likewise pure show; on the page the secret sleeps elsewhere.",
    ],
  },
  "Craster": {
    paras: [
      "Craster’s Keep is a midden with a hall on it, deep in the haunted forest — one aging wildling, a herd of daughter-wives, and no sons in sight. Craster himself is a bastard of the borderlands, wildling mother, crow father, raised in the Watch’s shadow and belonging to neither world; he has spent a lifetime building a third one, exactly big enough for himself. Within it he is king, priest and husband: he weds his daughters as they ripen, rules by fist and scripture of his own devising, and calls himself a godly man with the serene confidence of a man who has chosen gods that agree with him.",
      "The horror of the arrangement is its arithmetic. Daughters stay; sons go. Newborn boys are carried into the wood and left, and the cold gods — the white shadows, the wives whisper — come for them. The tale is careful never to show the transaction from Craster’s side, which makes it worse: whatever he believes he is buying — safety, favor, the right to be left alone while the world beyond his palisade empties — the price is paid in infants, and the keep’s survival through years that swallow whole villages suggests the customers are real and the contract honored.",
      "To the Night’s Watch he is that most corrosive of things, a necessary shame. The rangers call him a friend to the Watch; he sells them information and floor space, wintering patrols in a hall that stinks of what he is, and officers who would hang a brother for touching a wildling girl teach recruits not to look at Craster’s wives. Mormont’s Great Ranging shelters under his roof going north and staggers back to it broken — and the Old Bear’s courtesy to his host, maintained through gritted teeth, becomes the last straw for starving men who can smell the hidden larder.",
      "Craster is the tale’s bluntest argument about what institutions will tolerate to survive. The Watch knows. The wives’ whispers, the missing sons, the thing in the wood — the order that exists to fight the cold gods keeps a supplier of theirs as an ally, because his roof is warm and his information good, and every officer who winters there signs the ledger by saying nothing. When the reckoning arrives it comes not as justice but as squalor — hungry men, hidden bread, drawn knives — and takes the Watch’s honor down with the host’s.",
      "His legacy outlives him on both sides of the ledger. Gilly and her son — the one boy who got away — carry his blood south into the story’s gentlest family, raised on the opposite of everything the keep taught. And in the deep cold beyond, the tale strongly implies, the rest of Craster’s sons are still in service: the pale army assembling against the world was tithed, in part, from one man’s cradles. Few characters so minor cast a longer shadow.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show makes explicit what the books leave in whispers: an early episode follows a son of Craster’s into the wood and shows a White Walker carrying the child away, and a later one shows the transformation itself — the infant laid on an altar of ice, the Night King’s touch, the eyes turning blue. The books offer only the wives’ terrified hints and the keep’s unnatural safety, and their Craster is if anything meaner in scale — no altar, no throne room of ice, just a filthy old man with a hidden larder and an arrangement nobody dares examine. The mutiny differs in its details too: on the page the fatal insult is the word bastard flung by starving men, and it is the ranger Dirk who opens Craster’s throat, while the show gives the deed to Karl Tanner and lets the mutineers squat in the keep for half a season, an occupation the books never stage.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He dies under his own roof, by guest right’s ugliest breach in a saga full of them. With the shattered remnant of the Great Ranging starving in his hall, Craster plays host with a miser’s hand — boasting of his godliness while the Watch’s wounded die around a cold hearth — until the taunting and the hunger strike flint. In the books a brother calls him bastard, Craster comes up raging with an axe, and Dirk opens his throat over the bread he swore he didn’t have; on screen it is Karl Tanner who does it, driving a knife up through the old man’s mouth mid-sneer, with a toast to being a legend. The mutiny that murders him kills Lord Commander Mormont minutes later — and his wives, asked what should be done with them, tell the survivors to burn the dead and go. Nobody in either telling mourns him; the wood, presumably, noticed the tithe had stopped.",
    ],
  },
  "Mance Rayder": {
    paras: [
      "Mance Rayder is the Night’s Watch’s most gifted product and its most instructive failure. Wildling-born, taken as a child and raised at the Wall, he became one of the order’s finest rangers — clever, charming, a fighter, a singer — and then walked away. The books give his desertion the texture of a parable: mauled by a shadowcat, he was healed in a wildling hut by a woman who mended his torn cloak with scarlet silk, and when the Watch made him replace the mended cloak with a plain black one, he understood the bargain of his whole life. The Watch had given him everything except the right to own anything, and he went over the Wall to be free.",
      "What he built there had never been built before. The free folk are called free because no one commands them — dozens of peoples, mutually raiding, speaking different tongues, agreeing on nothing but their contempt for kneelers — and Mance welded them into one host: Thenns and Hornfoots, ice-river cannibals and cave dwellers, giants, wargs, and every raider chief with a grudge. He did it without a crown, a throne, or the power to behead anyone, which he is quick to point out makes him the only king in Westeros who is actually elected daily. His tools were persuasion, patience, a harp, and the one argument that beats ten thousand years of feuding: the dead are coming, and the Wall has only one warm side.",
      "That is the tale’s great irony about Mance: the King-Beyond-the-Wall is not invading, he is evacuating. His host drags families, herds and sledges toward the Wall because he has seen what stirs in the far north — his people are dying and rising in the cold, and every battle he fights against the Watch is a fight to put the Wall between his people and their own dead. The order he once served reads the largest migration in history as the assault it was built to stop, and the two sides bleed each other in front of the actual enemy.",
      "His duel of wits with Jon Snow structures the whole northern arc. Mance knows a planted turncloak when he sees one — he did the walk himself, in the other direction — and takes Jon in anyway, testing him against Ygritte, Tormund and the Magnar, teaching him the free folk from the inside. When the Watch’s trickery is finally spent and Stannis Baratheon’s knights shatter the wildling host beneath the Wall, Mance’s kingship ends the way it began: not with a betrayal by his people, who never stopped following him, but with a southern power deciding what to do with a king who will not kneel.",
      "Mance matters because he is the story’s working demonstration that the Wall’s two sides are one people separated by masonry. Ranger and raider, singer and general, kneeler and free — every division the saga draws in the north, Mance has lived on both sides of, and found both wanting. The free folk who eventually pass the Wall and fight the Long Night beside the Watch walk through a gate Mance Rayder spent his life arguing open, whatever name is on the treaty by then.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The tellings part company at the pyre, and the divergence is total. The show burns Mance in its fifth season’s first hour and means it; the books burn a glamoured Rattleshirt in his place, Melisandre’s ruby-magic dressing the Lord of Bones in the king’s features while the real Mance — hidden under the bone-lord’s rattling shirt — is quietly kept in service. Book-Mance then rides to Winterfell posing as the bard Abel, six spearwives at his back, to steal Ramsay Bolton’s bride out of the heart of the Bolton stronghold — one of the fifth book’s great covert operations, and it ends with the Pink Letter claiming he sits in a cage wearing a cloak of spearwives’ skins. Whether that letter tells the truth is one of the saga’s liveliest open questions; what is certain is that Mance Rayder is alive in the published books, and the show’s clean, honorable ending is an invention.",
      "The characters differ in flavor as well as fate. Book-Mance is a performer to the bone — he once climbed the Wall in a rebuilt cloak to walk Winterfell’s feast hall as a minstrel and look Robert Baratheon in the eye, purely because he could. The show pares away the theatricality and the harp and gives the essentials to a quieter, grimmer man whose greatness is all in his stillness — the version who tells Jon Snow the freedom to make his own mistakes was all he ever wanted.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "In the show he dies for a word. Stannis offers the captive King-Beyond-the-Wall his life for a bent knee and the free folk’s spears for a kneeler’s pledge, and Mance — courteous, regretful, entirely immovable — explains that the moment he kneels, everything he built is unbuilt. Melisandre gives him to her god before the whole watching Wall: bound to the pyre, he holds his composure until the flames climb, and it is Jon Snow’s arrow, loosed across the yard against every order, that reaches his heart before the fire can finish its work. The wildlings remember the mercy; Stannis, pointedly, remembers the disobedience.",
      "In the books the pyre is a magic trick. The man who burns screaming in Mance’s face and Mance’s chains is Rattleshirt, wrapped in Melisandre’s glamour; the true Mance survives as her hidden asset and is spent on the Winterfell infiltration, last reported — by Ramsay Bolton, hardly a disinterested witness — as a prisoner in a cage. Alive in the published books, his final fate unwritten: of all the divergences between page and screen, Mance’s is the widest still open.",
    ],
  },
  "Ygritte": {
    paras: [
      "Ygritte is the free folk’s whole argument in one red-haired package. A spearwife of no particular band and no rank — the wildlings have none to give — she is kissed by fire, quick with a bow, quicker with a jibe, and possessed of exactly one settled opinion about the boy crow captured in the Skirling Pass: that he knows nothing. The phrase starts as mockery, becomes flirtation, becomes love, and never once stops being true.",
      "She meets Jon Snow across a drawn blade — his — and survives because he cannot swing it. That failed execution is the hinge of both their stories: to Ygritte it is proof the crow has a man inside the cloak; to the wildlings it is a debt; and to Jon it is the first crack in the wall between his vows and his eyes. She repays it by vouching for him to Rattleshirt’s band and to Mance himself, staking her standing on a turncloak’s sincerity — which makes her, by the free folk’s hard accounting, responsible for him.",
      "What follows is the tale’s best-argued romance. In the cave beneath the earth, in the climb up the Wall’s impossible face, Ygritte teaches Jon the free folk from the inside — that they are not the raiders of Old Nan’s tales but a people, with songs and dead and a claim to the north older than the Starks — and un-teaches him most of what the Watch taught. Her creed is stated plainly: you are mine and I am yours, and if we die, we die, but first we’ll live. Jon, fatally, is never quite able to say the same words without hearing his vows underneath them.",
      "When he breaks — abandoning the raiding party at the Queenscrown rather than kill an old man for show — Ygritte’s heartbreak arrives as arrows. She puts three shafts in him as he flees, and the books and show agree on the essential ambiguity: a woman who could split a wallet at a hundred paces left every one of them out of the kill. Reunited at the storming of Castle Black on opposite sides, she fights her way through the Watch with his name in her teeth, the war and the love wound so tight together that not even she can say which one aimed her bow.",
      "Ygritte’s meaning outlives her: she is the face Jon Snow sees every time the Watch debates whether wildlings are people. The gates that open to the free folk, the alliance that holds the Long Night — the tale routes all of it through one spearwife who took a crow into a cave and told him he knew nothing. She was right, and he spent the rest of the saga learning what she knew.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show hands her death an author: Olly, the boy whose village her band destroyed, puts the arrow in her back in the burning yard of Castle Black — grief answering grief, and Jon holds her as she goes. The books are crueler by being vaguer: Jon finds her dying after the battle with a shaft through her chest, and the tale never says whose it was; he prays it wasn’t his, and gets no answer. Book-Ygritte is also a fraction more feral and more mythic — it is she who tells the tale of Bael the Bard and insists the Starks have wildling blood, and her jealous streak (the show’s cave idyll notwithstanding) runs hotter on the page. Both tellings agree on the essentials: the red hair, the creed, and the last line about the cave.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "She dies in Jon Snow’s arms in the wreck of the battle for Castle Black. On screen she has him at bowshot in the burning yard — and hesitates, the bow trembling between love and war, until Olly’s arrow takes her through the back. In the books Jon finds her among the wounded when the fighting ebbs, an arrow in her chest that no one will ever claim, her hair the last warm color in a yard full of ash. Both tellings give her the same dying words — that they should have stayed in that cave — and Jon the same answer, that he’ll take her back there. He keeps the promise the only way left: her body is burned north of the Wall, in the free air, as the free folk do, and the fire her hair was kissed by takes the rest of her home.",
    ],
  },
  "Tormund Giantsbane": {
    paras: [
      "Tormund’s titles arrive before he does: Giantsbane, Tall-talker, Horn-blower, Husband to Bears, Mead-king of Ruddy Hall, Father of Hosts — a résumé he composed himself and recites at the least provocation, each entry backed by a story that grows in the telling. He once slew a giant, or possibly slept in one’s fist through a winter; he suckled at a giantess, or bedded a bear, depending on the mead. The bragging is a wildling art form and Tormund its living master, but the saga’s joke runs the other way: strip every exaggeration and the remainder is still one of the most dangerous and most respected war-chiefs of the free folk.",
      "In Mance Rayder’s host he is the loyal center — commander of raiders, breaker of skeptics, the chief the King-Beyond-the-Wall trusts with the hard jobs and the honest counsel. It is Tormund who leads the party over the Wall to strike Castle Black from the south, Tormund who fights on when subtler men would treat, and Tormund who is dragged from the field full of arrows still trying to bite. His courage is not the grim northern kind; it is appetite — for fighting, for mead, for life at full volume — and it makes him the free folk’s best ambassador precisely because no one can meet him and keep believing wildlings are monsters.",
      "The alliance with Jon Snow is the unlikeliest friendship in the tale and one of its sturdiest. The crow who was Mance’s prisoner becomes the Lord Commander who unchains Tormund, and between them they do what ten thousand years of history said was impossible: bring the free folk through the Wall as allies. Tormund pays his share in full — he stands at Jon’s shoulder at Hardhome when the dead come over the palisade, sails back with the survivors, and puts the wildlings’ spears behind the Starks at the Battle of the Bastards, where he pulls down Smalljon Umber with his teeth in the man’s throat. Kneelers’ wars, he grumbles, and fights them anyway, because Jon asks.",
      "He survives everything the end of the world throws at him — the collapse of Eastwatch, the Long Night at Winterfell — and does it while conducting the saga’s most one-sided courtship, an epic, entirely unrequited passion for Brienne of Tarth that he narrates in looks of profound, mead-lit devotion. The comedy is load-bearing: in a story where the north’s survivors are counted on fingers, Tormund’s enormous appetite for living is the argument that survival is worth it.",
      "When the wars end he does what the free folk have always done — he goes home. The true north empties its refugees back through the Wall with Tormund at their head, and Jon Snow, exiled from every throne and title the south invented, rides out beside him. The last image of the wildling arc is Tormund leading his people into the green shoots beyond the Wall: the man who followed a king south, and finished the tale needing no king at all.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Book-Tormund is grayer, older and — the White God-Beyond-the-Wall forbid — slightly more modest; several of the show’s tallest tales are the screen turning his boasts up to eleven, and the Brienne infatuation is pure invention (they never meet on the page). His book role after Mance’s fall is weightier: it is Tormund who takes up leadership of the shattered free folk remnant, Tormund who negotiates the great passage through the Wall with Lord Commander Jon Snow — hostages, terms, the weeping of chiefs handing over sons — and Tormund who is standing at Jon’s side at Castle Black on the night of the knives. He is alive in the published books, unfinished business incarnate; the show’s Hardhome and Winterfell heroics are extrapolation the books have not yet reached.",
    ],
  },
  "Karsi": {
    paras: [
      "Karsi exists for one hour of the story and is remembered as if she’d had seven seasons — a free folk chieftain at Hardhome, mother of two girls, one of the elders the wildlings actually listen to when Jon Snow and Tormund arrive with their impossible offer of ships and southern refuge. She is everything the Watch’s propaganda says wildlings are not: reasonable, wry, quicker than the men around her to weigh a bad bargain against a worse one. Her word carries; when Karsi loads her daughters onto the boats, others follow.",
      "The genius of the character is economy. In a few minutes of screen time — a knife held on Jon, an argument won with Rattleshirt’s heckling, a joke about her ancestors’ opinion of crows, two small girls handed down into a rowboat — the show builds a whole life, precisely so that the storm about to hit Hardhome has a face the audience has decided to love. She is the episode’s thesis: these people the realm calls savages are the realm, and every one lost to the cold is a spear added to its army.",
      "When the dead come over the palisade Karsi fights like the chieftain she is — in the shield line, spear working, holding the retreat open while the boats load. The story gives her competence, courage and command right up to the moment it reveals what those are worth against the true enemy, which is, of course, the point of Hardhome: nothing human is enough.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Karsi is a pure show creation; no such chieftain appears in the books. The novels never visit Hardhome at all — the rescue mission is Cotter Pyke’s, launched by sea from Eastwatch, and everything the reader learns arrives in his laconic, ominous letters to Jon Snow: witches, wrecks, hunger, and dead things in the water. The show sailed the camera where the books sent only correspondence, invented Karsi to give the massacre a human center, and in doing so converted one of the saga’s great off-page dreads into its most celebrated battle hour. Her narrative function — the sympathetic wildling parent whose fate indicts the audience’s own detachment — is the show doing in one episode what the books do across Jon’s whole Wall arc.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "She dies at Hardhome, in the battle she almost survived. With her daughters safe on the boats and the palisade fallen, Karsi cuts her way toward the shore — and comes face to face with a pack of wight children, blue-eyed and small as her own girls. The spear that never hesitated hesitates; she cannot strike them, and they swarm her down. The episode’s final cruelty is precise: the Night King raises the field’s dead with a slow lift of his arms, and Karsi stands up again among them, eyes burning blue — the mother who saved her children conscripted into the army that will hunt them. No death in the show argues the stakes of the war more efficiently.",
    ],
  },
  "Styr": {
    paras: [
      "The Thenns are the free folk’s paradox: the most disciplined, most law-bound, most nearly civilized of the peoples beyond the Wall — and for exactly that reason, the most feared. In their high mountain valley in the far north they mine bronze, forge scaled armor, and obey their lord absolutely, for the Magnar of Thenn is more god than chief. Styr holds that title, and wears it like his people wear their bronze: without ornament and without doubt. Earless, scarred, humorless, he commands the closest thing Mance Rayder’s patchwork host has to regular infantry.",
      "That is why Mance gives him the hardest assignment of the war: take a picked band over the Wall itself and fall on Castle Black from its undefended southern side when the main host strikes the north face. Styr’s column — with Jon Snow embedded in it as a very unconvincing turncloak — climbs the ice, raids down through the Gift, and announces itself in slaughtered villages. It is Styr who sets Jon the test of killing an innocent old man, and Styr who is proven right about the crow when Jon breaks and flees at the Queenscrown.",
      "As a character he is the free folk with the charm removed — a reminder, carefully placed beside Ygritte and Tormund, that the world beyond the Wall contains tyrants of its own, and that freedom from kneeling is not freedom from power. The Magnar kneels to no southern king because his people already kneel to him.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show reinvented the Thenns wholesale. The books’ Thenns are the least savage of the free folk — bronze-armored, Old Tongue-speaking, so orderly that the Watch later marries their new Magnar, Styr’s son Sigorn, to Alys Karstark, founding a house of Thenn kneelers in the north’s heartland. The screen’s Thenns are scarified, bald, and eat their enemies — cannibalism borrowed from the ice-river clans to give season four a memorable villain, with Styr rebuilt to match as a leering brute. Book-Styr is no monster, merely absolute; his quarrel with Jon is professional, not sadistic. The two versions share a name, a mission and a death at Castle Black, and almost nothing else.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "Both tellings kill him at Castle Black, in the assault he led. On screen he gets a villain’s exit: breaking through the gate with his Thenns, he beats Jon Snow half to death in the yard — swordless, outmatched, spitting — until Jon’s hand closes on a smith’s hammer and drives it up through the Magnar’s skull. The books end him with the Wall itself: leading the escalade against the castle’s southern face, Styr and his climbers meet the Watch’s prepared defenses on the ice, and the great stair collapses in fire and frozen thunder, taking the Magnar down with tons of ice — the mountain lord killed by the only thing in the south taller than his mountains. His people’s story continues without him in the books, wearing Karstark colors.",
    ],
  },
  "Val": {
    paras: [
      "Val enters the story as an afterthought of grief: sister to Dalla, the wife of Mance Rayder who dies giving birth to his son the same night Stannis’s knights shatter the wildling host. The southerners who take her captive look at her — tall, fair, striking — and reach for the only word their world has for a king’s beautiful sister-in-law: princess. The free folk find this hilarious. Val belongs to no one and confers no claim, and the tale mines a rich seam of comedy and menace from the parade of kneeler lords who court her as though she were a title deed with braids.",
      "Captivity does not much dent her. Held at Castle Black as a hostage to wildling good behavior, she keeps her own counsel, tends her dead sister’s newborn, and studies her captors with the patience of a hunter in a blind. Stannis proposes to dispose of her hand as a reward for his lords; Val’s own position, expressed with total clarity, is that a man who wants her should be warned she has knife skills and opinions. Jon Snow, who deals with her as an envoy rather than a prize, gets the measure of her better than the kings do.",
      "Her great errand proves the point. When Jon needs Tormund’s wandering remnant found and brought to terms — thousands of starving free folk, somewhere in the haunted forest, with winter closing — it is Val he sends, alone, over the Wall on a garron with no escort and no guarantee she will ever come back. She rides out like the north made flesh, keeps her word, and returns with the job done: the so-called captive princess accomplishing, unarmed and unaccompanied, what the Watch could not do with ranging parties.",
      "Val’s sharpest scene is a warning. Meeting Shireen Baratheon, she recoils and tells Jon plainly that beyond the Wall the girl’s greyscale would mark her for death — that the free folk know the grey death sleeps and wakes, and kill the touched before it wakes. It lands as superstition and reads, in the books’ gathering pattern, like foreshadowing the south is too civilized to hear. That is Val’s function in one line: she says the things the free folk know and the kneelers have forgotten, and the story keeps quietly proving her right.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Val does not exist in the show. Her narrative estate was broken up and distributed: the wildling princess Stannis’s camp wants to marry off vanishes entirely; the hostage-envoy work goes to Tormund; and Mance’s wife and son — the reason for her presence at the Wall — are cut with her, which is why the show’s Mance can burn with no heir for Melisandre to covet and no baby for Jon to smuggle. Book-readers rate her among the most vivid characters the adaptation dropped: a point-of-view-less figure who nonetheless steals every chapter she enters, last seen alive and at liberty at Castle Black — where, on the night of the mutiny, she remains one of the fifth book’s many loaded, unfired guns. She is alive in the published books.",
    ],
  },
  "Wun Wun": {
    paras: [
      "Wun Weg Wun Dar Wun — Wun Wun, to everyone whose tongue gives out — is one of the last giants in the world, and the story’s walking elegy for everything the world is losing. The giants of the true north are not the ogres of southern tales but an old, dwindling people: mammoth-riders, Old Tongue-speakers, gentler than their size until pushed, who have been retreating before men since men arrived. By the tale’s time they number in the dozens, all of them inside Mance Rayder’s host, refugees like everyone else from the cold that is eating their world.",
      "Wun Wun comes south with the free folk remnant and becomes, improbably, a fixture of the Wall’s garrison — a fourteen-foot ally with a taste for anything resembling food and a temper that surfaces exactly when armed men forget their manners. The sworn brothers and the kneeler lords gawk, and the tale enjoys the reversal: the monster of legend turns out to be the most straightforwardly honest creature at Castle Black, dangerous only to people who richly earn it.",
      "His meaning is written in his rarity. Every other faction in the saga can replace its losses; the giants cannot. When Wun Wun fights — and he fights like a siege engine with a grievance — the audience is watching a species spend itself for allies who, two years earlier, would have hunted it. The free folk understood what the realm learns late: when the giants are gone, they are gone, and the world that comes after will be smaller in every sense.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The tellings deploy him differently. Book-Wun Wun is a Castle Black character: recruited from the refugees, lodged in the castle, and central to the fifth book’s explosive final scene — when the Watch’s tensions detonate, it is Wun Wun who tears apart the queen’s knight Ser Patrek in the yard, the chaos into which the mutineers’ knives come out for Jon Snow. He is alive in the published books, roaring over a dead knight as the story cuts to black. The show marched him further: through the massacre at Hardhome, over the wall of Castle Black to end the mutineers’ standoff, and into the Stark vanguard at the Battle of the Bastards — a military career the books have not yet written.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "In the show the last giant dies buying Winterfell back for the Starks. At the Battle of the Bastards, Wun Wun anchors the wildling line through the slaughter, survives the arrow-storm and the crush, and when the survivors reach Winterfell’s sealed gates he breaks them open with his own body — shouldering through the oak and iron until the way is clear and he can no longer stand. Feathered with shafts and bolts, kneeling in the breach he made, he looks up as Ramsay Bolton puts a final arrow in his eye. Jon Snow reaches him in time to watch him fall; the death goes unavenged for approximately ninety seconds. With him, the show quietly notes, the age of giants ends. He is alive in the published books.",
    ],
  },
  "Bowen Marsh": {
    paras: [
      "Bowen Marsh is the man the Wall’s ledgers would elect. Lord Steward of the Night’s Watch, keeper of its granaries, counter of its barrels — the brothers call him the Old Pomegranate, round and red and seedy with figures — he is the indispensable administrator of an order that romanticizes its rangers and runs on its clerks. Every winter the Watch survives, it survives partly because Bowen Marsh counted the turnips honestly. The tale takes him seriously precisely because institutions are wrecked as often by their accountants as by their enemies.",
      "His virtues and his limits are the same trait viewed from two sides: Marsh believes in the Watch as it has always been. Wildlings are the enemy — he has the scars and the casualty rolls to prove it, having bled fighting them at the Bridge of Skulls — and the Wall’s purpose is to keep them out. When command passes to a young Lord Commander who opens the gates to those same wildlings, feeds them from Marsh’s precious stores, and begins entangling the Watch in the realm’s wars, the Old Pomegranate’s objections arrive first as arithmetic, then as counsel, then as increasingly desperate warning.",
      "The books build his arc as a tragedy of the reasonable man. Marsh is not cruel, not ambitious, not even wrong about the numbers; he is a conservative steward watching everything he understands be spent on a gamble he cannot see the end of. Each escalation — the free folk through the gates, the loan from the Iron Bank, the plan to march on Winterfell — moves him one step from grumbling toward something colder. The horror of where he ends up is that every step is defensible in committee.",
      "His meaning in the saga is the question he embodies: what does loyalty to an institution mean when the institution’s purpose changes under your feet? Marsh’s answer — that the Watch he swore to must be preserved even from its own commander — is the dark twin of Jon Snow’s, and the books are honest enough to let him believe he is the faithful one. For the Watch, he says, and the tale leaves the reader to weigh whether that is a confession or a creed.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show reduced him to a background face and reassigned his knife. On the page it is Bowen Marsh, weeping, who leads the officers of the Watch in the assassination of Jon Snow — the fifth book’s final convulsion, driven by the stewards and the old guard, men whose grievances are institutional rather than personal. The screen gave that conspiracy to Alliser Thorne’s bitterness and Olly’s grief, faces the audience knew, and left Marsh unnamed in the crowd. The change trades the books’ chillier point — that Jon is killed not by his enemies but by the Watch’s reasonable middle — for a more legible betrayal. Marsh has no death in either telling: he is alive in the published books, holding Castle Black in the aftermath of what he did, with Wun Wun’s roars and the free folk’s numbers arrayed against the arithmetic of his survival.",
    ],
  },
  "Cotter Pyke": {
    paras: [
      "Cotter Pyke is what the Night’s Watch does with talent that arrives without a name. Baseborn on the Iron Islands — his surname is the one the ironborn stamp on bastards — he came to the Wall rough and stayed rough, and rose anyway: command of Eastwatch-by-the-Sea, the Watch’s port and fleet, held by a man who cannot comfortably read the letters his maester writes for him. The brothers who sneer at his birth do it quietly and from a distance, because Cotter Pyke has never lost a fight he could reach.",
      "The books pair him with his opposite number for one of the Watch’s best running jokes: Ser Denys Mallister of the Shadow Tower, silver-haired, courteous, impeccably born — and the two commanders detest each other with a completeness that turns every council into a diplomatic incident. During the drawn-out election of a new Lord Commander, their rival candidacies deadlock the Watch for days, a stalemate broken only when Sam Tarly plays the two old enemies against each other to clear the path for Jon Snow. Pyke takes the outcome the way he takes everything: profanely, and then back to work.",
      "His great assignment is the saga’s most dreaded errand. When word comes that thousands of free folk are starving at Hardhome with the dead closing in, it is Cotter Pyke who takes eleven ships north to bring them off — the Watch doing its true work at its farthest reach. What comes back is not a fleet but a correspondence: terse, misspelled, increasingly grim letters that read like a horror story told by a man too stubborn to raise his voice, ending with a plea for help by land and the line readers quote with a shiver — dead things in the woods, dead things in the water.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show cut him and split the inheritance. Eastwatch-by-the-Sea survives on screen — handed to Tormund to garrison, and demolished by the ice dragon — but its foul-mouthed commander does not, and his Hardhome mission is transferred wholesale to Jon Snow and Tormund so the audience can sail with it; Karsi and the massacre episode occupy the space the books fill with Pyke’s letters. The election farce with Ser Denys is likewise gone, folded into a quicker show of hands. Book-readers keep a wary eye on his story: as the published saga stands he is alive — or at least unaccounted for — somewhere north of the Wall at Hardhome, at the end of a paper trail that stops mid-plea. The Watch has heard nothing since, and neither have we.",
    ],
  },
  "Rast": {
    paras: [
      "Rast is the recruit the Wall was invented for: a rapist offered the black instead of the knife, who arrives at Castle Black having learned nothing from the reprieve. In Thorne’s training yard he finds the natural occupation of the strong and stupid — tormenting Samwell Tarly — until Jon Snow ends the sport by other means: Rast wakes one night to a direwolf’s weight on his chest and a quiet promise, and Sam’s troubles in the yard stop that day. He never forgives either of them, and files the grievance where men like Rast file everything, with interest.",
      "The Great Ranging carries him beyond the Wall with the rest, and hunger and fear finish what resentment started. At Craster’s Keep he is in the thick of the mutiny — among the first knives out when the starving remnant turns on its host and its commander — and afterward he stays on with the mutineers to enjoy the spoils of the smallest, ugliest kingdom in the world: a longhall, a larder, and Craster’s captive wives.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Book-Rast is a training-yard bully who fades from the page after the recruits are assigned — the Ghost-on-the-chest warning is his one memorable book scene, and he takes no recorded part in the mutiny, whose named knives are Dirk, Ollo Lophand, Clubfoot Karl and their like. He has no death in the published books; he simply drops out of the story, one more black brother somewhere in the ranks. The show promoted him into the mutiny’s second face — it is Rast who actually stabs the Old Bear on screen — and gave him a season of squalid tyranny at the keep, largely so that the audience would have a familiar name to hate when the Watch came calling.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "The show settles his account with tidy cruelty. When Jon Snow leads the loyalists back to Craster’s Keep, Rast — who put his knife in Jeor Mormont’s back and spent the months since starving a caged direwolf for amusement — runs from the fight and makes the treeline. Ghost is waiting there. The wolf he tormented takes his throat, and the man who began the story raping and ended it stabbing commanders dies alone in the dark, mourned by no one, his death scene shorter than his crimes deserve. In the published books he is alive, or at least unaccounted for — the page never bothered to finish him.",
    ],
  },
  "Karl Tanner": {
    paras: [
      "Karl Tanner introduces himself as a legend — a fookin’ legend, of Gin Alley, in Flea Bottom — and the alarming thing is that the boast checks out. A paid knifeman from the capital’s worst streets, he brought to the Night’s Watch the skills of the Flea Bottom cutthroat: speed, viciousness, and a total, liberating absence of scruple. The Wall took him as it takes everyone, and discovered too late that some men treat vows the way they treat locks — as an inconvenience between them and the goods.",
      "The mutiny at Craster’s Keep is his masterpiece and his coronation. It is Karl who turns the starving remnant’s grievance into murder — taunting Craster to his feet and putting a dirk up through his skull mid-boast — and Karl who rules the keep afterward, drinking wine from the Old Bear’s skull and presiding over a court of deserters with Craster’s wives as its currency. His speech about being better than wellborn commanders — he was killing for pay in King’s Landing while they played with wooden swords — is the mutiny’s whole philosophy: the Watch’s discipline was only ever fear, and beyond the Wall the fear points the other way.",
      "He matters as the tale’s demonstration of what the Watch is holding back inside its own walls. The order recruits from dungeons and calls the result brothers; Karl Tanner is the residue that oath-making cannot dissolve, the reminder that some of the swords guarding the realms of men were pointed at men for money the week before. When the Watch returns to burn the keep, it is not avenging Mormont so much as auditing itself.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Karl Tanner as the screen knows him is a show creation — the name, the Gin Alley legend, the skull goblet. The books’ mutiny has no single ringleader with his profile: Craster dies to the ranger Dirk’s knife in a spontaneous brawl over hidden food, the Old Bear to a tangle of hands, and the mutineers who squat at the keep afterward are a rabble — Dirk, Ollo Lophand, Clubfoot Karl (the name’s book-cousin), Garth of Greenaway and the rest — with no court and no philosophy. Nor does the Watch return to punish them: in the books that reckoning belongs to a stranger errand, as Coldhands hunts the mutineers through the haunted forest on Bran Stark’s behalf and leaves what remains for the crows. The show consolidated the rabble into one memorable villain and gave the Watch the vengeance the books outsourced.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He dies in the retaking of Craster’s Keep, in the best fight the show’s Wall arc had staged to that point. When Jon Snow comes through the door, Karl meets him with Flea Bottom rules — two knives, spat insults, dirty feints — and is winning, cutting the swordsman apart at close quarters, when one of Craster’s wives buries a blade in his back for every night of his reign. He turns on her, and Jon finishes it: a sword driven through the back of Karl Tanner’s skull and out his mouth, ending the legend of Gin Alley mid-sneer. The keep is burned with his body in it. Having never existed in the books, he has no fate there to await.",
    ],
  },
  "Olly": {
    paras: [
      "Olly is the war seen from below the age of enlistment. A smallfolk boy from a village north of the last northern lords’ protection, he is feeding chickens one morning when Styr’s raiders come over the hill; by nightfall he has watched the Thenns butcher his parents and his neighbors, and been left alive on purpose — told to run to Castle Black and tell the crows what is coming. The free folk meant him as a message. The Watch takes him in as a mouth to feed, and he stays as something between a recruit and a foundling, learning the bow he could not draw the day it mattered.",
      "The battle for Castle Black makes him a soldier at perhaps twelve years old — and gives him the arrow that history will remember him for, loosed from the yard into the back of the spearwife aiming at Jon Snow. That the spearwife was Ygritte, and that Jon loved her, is a thing Olly cannot know and would not weigh; from where he stands she was one of the raiders who laughed while his village burned. Afterward Jon makes the boy his steward, as Mormont once made Jon — a kindness with an old design, the Watch turning grief into service.",
      "But the design fails, and the failing is the point of him. Olly watches the man he serves — the commander he half-worships — open the gates to the people who murdered his family, feed them, settle them, and ask the Watch to fight beside them. Every argument about the dead beyond the Wall runs off the boy like rain off slate; he was not orphaned by White Walkers. His face, watching the free folk file through the gate, is the mutiny in miniature long before any officer says the words: some debts do not care what is coming.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Olly is a show invention with a precise job: to give a face and a comprehensible grief to what the books distribute across an institution. On the page, no boy shoots Ygritte — her arrow is anonymous — and no child stands among Jon’s assassins; the conspiracy is grown men of the Watch’s old guard, stewards and officers led by Bowen Marsh, weeping as they strike for the Watch. The show gathered all that institutional resentment into one small, wounded figure the audience had watched grow up, so that the final knife would come from the last hand Jon — or the viewer — could hate. It worked well enough that Olly became one of the most reviled characters in the adaptation, which is its own tribute: he is the Watch’s grudge given a child’s face, and children are harder to forgive.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He ends at the end of a rope, and the show does not flinch from it. Olly delivers the last knife in the mutiny — luring Jon into the yard with a lie about his uncle, then stepping through the ring of officers to drive the final dagger in with tears on his face — and when Jon Snow returns from the dead, the boy hangs beside Thorne and the other conspirators, the youngest neck on the beam. He offers no last words; he just stares at Jon until the box is kicked away. Jon cuts the bodies down himself, and it is Olly’s face — the steward, the orphan, the mutineer, still a child — that ends his time as Lord Commander: he hands Edd his cloak and rides away from the Watch that made them both. There is no Olly in the books to meet any fate.",
    ],
  },
  "Rattleshirt": {
    paras: [
      "The Lord of Bones announces himself before you see him: a clatter of dead men’s ribs and giants’ knuckles, sewn over boiled leather and topped with a broken giant’s-skull helm. The free folk name him for the armor — Rattleshirt to his face if you are brave, the Lord o’ Bones in the songs — and he has built a raider’s reputation to match, loud, vicious, and endlessly boastful of the corpses that supplied his wardrobe. Among a people whose only rank is reputation, Rattleshirt’s bones are his patent of nobility, and he defends them with a jealousy any southern lord would recognize.",
      "It is Rattleshirt’s war band that closes the trap in the Skirling Pass, and Rattleshirt who takes custody of the tale’s most consequential prisoner: Jon Snow, the crow who claims to want to turn his cloak. He argues loudly for killing him — his standing instinct in all ambiguous cases — and is overruled by Ygritte’s vouching and Mance’s curiosity, a decision he resents down every mile of the march. The tale uses him as the free folk’s id: where Mance calculates and Tormund laughs, Rattleshirt simply wants what raiders want, and his sneering vigil over Jon keeps the stakes of the infiltration honest.",
      "After the wildling host shatters beneath the Wall, the Lord of Bones ends up where all the broken pieces do — a captive of the kneelers, bones and bluster confiscated, reduced from war-chief to inventory. It is the free folk’s defeat in one image: the man who wore his kills as armor, standing in a pen wearing someone else’s mercy.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show keeps the bones and discards nearly everything else. Screen-Rattleshirt is a minor heavy — Jon’s captor in the Skirling Pass, a heckler at Hardhome — while book-Rattleshirt is loaded with one of the saga’s strangest destinies: after the battle, Melisandre’s ruby glamour swaps his seeming with Mance Rayder’s, and it is Rattleshirt who burns in Mance’s shape before the assembled Wall while the true king stands by wearing the bone-lord’s rattling shirt. For weeks afterward, the man everyone at Castle Black despises as Rattleshirt — sparring brutally with Jon, sneering from under the giant’s skull — is Mance Rayder in disguise, one of the books’ best-hidden tricks. The show, having no glamour plot, spent the character as crowd color and gave him a quick, crowd-pleasing exit at Tormund’s hands.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "His two deaths could not be less alike. In the books he dies in another man’s skin: condemned in Mance Rayder’s place, wrapped in Melisandre’s glamour, the Lord of Bones burns in a cage before the Wall screaming under a king’s face, and not even his executioners know whom the fire is actually eating. It is among the crueler deaths in the saga — anonymous by design, his last agony credited to a man he hated. The show’s version is almost merciful by comparison: at Hardhome, when Rattleshirt jeers once too often at the crows’ peace offer and calls Tormund a traitor, Tormund takes the bone-lord’s own staff from him and beats him to death with it in front of the assembled chieftains, then asks if anyone else objects. Nobody does; the meeting proceeds over his body.",
    ],
  },
  "Orell": {
    paras: [
      "Orell is a skinchanger — one of the free folk’s wargs, born with the old blood that lets a man slip his skin and ride a beast’s — and his second self is an eagle. That choice of bird tells you the man: not a wolf’s pack-loyalty or a hound’s devotion but a raptor’s cold altitude, watching everything, belonging to nothing. In Mance Rayder’s host he serves as the eyes of the war, his eagle riding the winds over the Wall and the Gift, counting crows and mapping the kill.",
      "In the wildling column he is Jon Snow’s most persistent doubter — with reason, as events prove. Orell reads the turncloak the way his eagle reads ground: he sees the crow under the sheepskin, says so early and often, and finds himself overruled by Ygritte’s heart and Mance’s gamble. The show sharpens the triangle by giving Orell his own designs on Ygritte, so that his hatred of Jon runs on two rails at once — the spy he distrusts and the rival he resents — and lets him force the issue at the climb, cutting the rope line and leaving Jon and Ygritte to fall, which they conspicuously decline to do.",
      "Orell’s deeper function is to carry the tale’s warg lore into the wildling arc: through him the story establishes what skinchanging is, what it costs, and what the free folk believe waits at the end of it — that a warg who dies in his man’s body lives on inside the beast, for a while, as a fading passenger. It is a doctrine the story states almost casually over a campfire and then spends, with terrible interest, elsewhere.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Timing is the great divergence. Book-Orell is dead before the reader ever properly meets him — killed by Jon Snow in the fight in the Skirling Pass in the second book — and exists thereafter as a legacy: his eagle, inherited in part by the warg Varamyr Sixskins, hates Jon with a dead man’s hatred and marks him for the host. The show resurrected him into a full speaking role — a season of scouting, suspicion and rivalry with a face and a name — essentially dramatizing in person the grudge the books conduct through a bird. His campfire warg-lore likewise absorbs material the books assign to Varamyr’s prologue. One detail survives every version: an eagle with a man inside it, circling Jon Snow, wanting him dead.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "Both tellings kill the man by Jon Snow’s hand and let the bird carry the grudge. In the books he dies in the Skirling Pass skirmish, cut down by Longclaw before Jon is ever taken into the host — and his eagle flies on with a remnant of Orell riding it, screaming over every battlefield of the wildling war until (the fifth book’s prologue reports) Melisandre’s fires burn it out of the sky beneath the Wall, killing him a second time and searing the warg who had taken up the reins. The show stages his death at the Queenscrown: when Jon refuses the Thenns’ test and the raiding party turns on him, Orell attacks and dies on Jon’s sword — slipping into his eagle as he falls, and spending his last seconds of manhood raking Jon’s face with the bird’s talons, the scars of which Jon carries for the rest of the tale. Skinchangers die twice, the free folk say; Orell manages to make both count.",
    ],
  },
  "The Three-Eyed Raven": {
    paras: [
      "Far north of the Wall, under a hill webbed with weirwood roots, sits the closest thing the world has to a memory. The Three-Eyed Raven is — or was — a man: a greenseer of enormous gift who went into the earth generations ago and let the trees grow through him, trading flesh for reach. From his root-woven throne he sees through every weirwood face in the world, forward and backward along time’s whole length, a thousand thousand moments at once. The children of the forest tend him like a relic; the ravens roost on him like a tree; and for years he has been reaching into the dreams of a broken boy in Winterfell, calling him north.",
      "His courtship of Bran Stark is conducted entirely in symbols: a three-eyed bird in fever dreams, promises of flight offered to a boy who will never walk. When Bran finally completes the terrible journey to the cave — past the Wall, past the dead, at the cost of lives spent along the way — he finds not a sorcerer but a ruin with eyes, a pale figure grown into the roots who tells him, with the gentleness of the very old, that he will never walk again, but he will fly. What follows is the strangest apprenticeship in the story: lessons conducted inside the past itself, teacher and student watching history like theater from the wings.",
      "The curriculum is memory, and the discipline is restraint. Through the weirwood’s eyes Bran visits Winterfell decades gone — his father young, his aunt laughing, the tower and the promise that secretly underpin the whole saga — and learns the rules by inches: the past is written, the ink is dry, and a greenseer who lingers too long or reaches too far can drown in the sea of time. The old man teaches with the urgency of a man reading his own hourglass; he has held the world’s memory for a very long while, and what he needs above all is not a pupil but a successor.",
      "Because the memory is contested. The Raven’s long war — waged in visions, across centuries, from a chair he cannot leave — is with the force beyond the cold that would like the world’s memory extinguished; the tale frames the Long Night not only as a war on the living but on the record of the living, and the Raven as its archivist under siege. Every lesson he crams into Bran is a book carried out of a burning library. His tragedy is the teacher’s oldest: the successor is not ready, and the enemy does not wait.",
      "What he means to the story is stated best by what replaces him. The boy who limps out of that cave is no longer quite Bran Stark; he is becoming what the old man was — the world’s memory in a young body, seeing everything, wanting almost nothing. Whether that transformation is a gift, a haunting, or a quiet abduction is one of the tale’s deliberately unsettled questions, and it inherits all the ambiguity of the figure in the roots: the Three-Eyed Raven saved the world’s past, and the price was two men’s futures.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The books give the figure a name and one of the saga’s richest backstories: he is Brynden Rivers, called Bloodraven — a bastard son of King Aegon the Fourth, albino, one-eyed, sorcerer, spymaster, Hand of the King, the most feared man of his age, who ended a rebellion with treachery at a peace banquet, was sent to the Wall for it, rose to Lord Commander of the Night’s Watch, and vanished on a ranging decades before the tale begins. A thousand eyes and one, the riddle-songs call him; finding that dreaded historical figure grown into a tree is one of the books’ great slow-fuse revelations, and it colors every lesson — Bran’s teacher is not a neutral sage but the most cunning political operator of his century, wearing roots. The show never names him, splits the role between two actors, and invents both his death and the Night King’s personal vendetta; the books’ version has no mark on Bran’s arm, no cave-fall — and is alive in the published books, still in his chair, still teaching.",
      "The tellings also disagree about the bird. In the books he is the three-eyed crow, and his dream-summons are stranger and sparser; the show’s rebranding to raven tidily matches the Westerosi post-bird and quietly drops the books’ implication — still argued over by readers — that the crow of Bran’s dreams and the man in the cave may not be exactly the same thing.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "The show kills him with a touch. When Bran, unsupervised in a vision, is found by the Night King and marked on the arm, the cave’s protections are voided at a stroke — the dead know where the world’s memory lives, and come for it. The Three-Eyed Raven spends his last hours in the only currency he has: he drags Bran into a final vision of old Winterfell and pours the succession into him mid-dream, teacher and archive at once, while outside the children of the forest and Summer and Hodor buy minutes with their lives. The Night King enters the cave and shears the old man apart at the root-throne; in the vision, Bran watches his teacher dissolve into wind and snow with the work unfinished — the world’s memory handed off mid-sentence, which explains a great deal about its new custodian.",
      "In the published books he has no death: Bloodraven sits in his roots still, Bran’s education barely begun, the Night King who kills him on screen not existing on the page at all. Readers of a certain temperament note that a man who was born in one king’s bed, died to the realm’s knowledge at the Wall, and turned up a century later inside a tree has already outlived three or four endings, and may decline this one too.",
    ],
  },
  "Leaf": {
    paras: [
      "Leaf is one of the last of the children of the forest — the small, dappled, golden-eyed people who were singing in Westeros ten thousand years before the First Men brought bronze and axes across the Arm of Dorne. The children fought the newcomers, made peace on the Isle of Faces, taught the old gods to the men who had come to kill them, and then spent a hundred centuries diminishing — retreating north, then beyond the Wall, then under it, into the hollow hill where the last greenseer sits in his roots. Leaf is among the few who remain: a being the size of a child and older than most human houses, tending the world’s memory in the dark.",
      "To Bran Stark’s ragged company she is rescue, guide and gentle disillusionment in one. It is Leaf who meets them at the cave mouth with fire against the dead, Leaf who feeds them and answers what may be asked, and Leaf who delivers, in her strange singing accent, the elegy of her whole race: the children are not hiding, they are ending — going out slowly, like an ember, while the world that replaced them forgets they were ever real. The tale gives its oldest people its quietest voice, and lets her matter-of-fact sorrow do what no battle scene could: make ten thousand years of loss feel like a household grief.",
      "The show hands her one more burden — authorship. In a vision of the Dawn Age, Bran watches Leaf and her kin bind a captive First Man to a weirwood and press dragonglass into his heart, making the first White Walker: a weapon, she admits when he confronts her, forged in desperation when men were cutting down the children’s trees and the children’s throats. It is the saga’s bleakest irony delivered in its smallest voice — the world’s ending was invented by its first people, in self-defense, and has outlived every war it was made for.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Book-Leaf carries the same elegy with different particulars. On the page she has walked in the lands of men — sent out young into the world beyond the cave, which is why her Common Tongue is fluent where her kin sing only the True Tongue — and she is emphatic that the children do not call themselves children at all; the name is men’s, and their own means those who sing the song of earth. Above all, the books never make her a weaponsmith: the Others’ origin remains a genuine mystery in the published novels, with no scene of dragonglass and no confession, and many readers doubt the show’s creation story reflects the books’ eventual answer. Book-Leaf is alive in the published story, still keeping the cave with her dwindling kin; her death in the tunnel, like the raid that causes it, is the screen’s invention.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "The show gives the last of the first people a rearguard’s death. When the Night King’s host breaches the greenseer’s hollow hill, Leaf and her remaining kin hold the tunnels with fire and obsidian while Bran is dragged toward the daylight — a species’ final battle fought in a corridor, buying seconds apiece. Cornered at the last with the wights closing from every side, Leaf lets them come: she waits until the dead are on her, a fire-charge clenched in her fist, and vanishes in the blast, spending the last of herself to seal the passage behind the survivors. The creature that made the first White Walker dies unmaking a few of his soldiers — ten thousand years of regret discharged in a single light. She is alive in the published books.",
    ],
  },
  "The Night King": {
    paras: [
      "The Night King is the show’s answer to a question the books leave in mist: what if the cold had a face? First glimpsed as a horned silhouette lifting a baby from an altar of ice, he is gradually revealed as the first and master of the White Walkers — a being of pale crystal calm who commands the dead the way weather commands leaves. He carries no sword he needs, speaks not one word in eight seasons, and is played entirely through stillness: an enemy whose most frightening habit is standing quietly at the back of a massacre, watching the living count their dead — and then raising his arms.",
      "His origin, shown in Bran Stark’s visions, is the saga’s bleakest piece of history: he was a man once, a First Man, captured by the children of the forest in their losing war and transformed — dragonglass driven into his heart at a weirwood — into a weapon against humanity. The weapon worked, then turned. Made to defend the singers’ world, he became the ending of all worlds, marching against children and makers alike; the show’s cosmology is a chain of desperate measures, and he is the first link, wrought by the desperate and no longer answering to anyone.",
      "What he wants is stated only by his enemies, but the show builds the case carefully: an endless night, and the death of memory. His fixation is Bran — the Three-Eyed Raven, the world’s living record — and the logic, as the living work it out in Winterfell’s war room, is total: kill the memory of the world and the world is over in a way no army can reverse. It reframes every horror behind him. The wights are not the point; the point is erasure, and everything else — the marches, the massacres, the spiral sigils his servants leave like signatures — is the patient administration of forgetting.",
      "His campaign is a masterpiece of cold patience. At Hardhome he lets the living load their boats, walks to the pier’s end, and raises the freshly slain by the thousand while Jon Snow watches — recruitment as taunt. Beyond the Wall he spends one javelin to bring down a dragon, then hauls Viserion from the frozen lake and opens the creature’s burning blue eye: the moment the war is lost, for the Wall was built against armies, not against a dead dragon’s fire. When Eastwatch falls in a cascade of ancient ice, eight thousand years of safety end in an evening, and the Long Night walks south on schedule.",
      "He is also the show’s great heresy of expectation. Every rule of the saga said the final enemy would be met by a destined sword — a prince promised, a hero reborn, a dragonlord queen. Instead the Night King is granted a single expression in his entire existence — a faint smile, standing unburnt in Drogon’s flame — and is ended not by prophecy’s appointed hand but by the story’s smallest knife arriving from outside every prediction. His whole arc is a lesson the show teaches about its own genre: the apocalypse does not read the songs.",
      "What he means is argued about precisely because he says nothing. He is death as a system rather than a character — recruitment without persuasion, victory without triumph, an enemy that converts every loss you suffer into his gain. Against a story otherwise powered by talk, want and memory, the show set a figure with no voice, no appetite and no past worth keeping, and let the contrast state the theme: everything in the tale that schemes and loves and remembers is what he is not, and what he came to end.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "There is no Night King in the published books. The Others of the page are a species, not a hierarchy — beautiful, terrible figures of mist and crystal who speak in voices like cracking ice, duel with blades that shatter steel, and appear in person only a handful of times in five volumes; no throne, no general, no chained dragon, and their purposes are entirely unrevealed. The show invented a commander to give its long war a face and a kill condition — and, in the touch-mark on Bran’s arm and the hunt for the Three-Eyed Raven, a personal plot the books show no sign of. Readers hunting his book-shadow find only legend: the Night’s King (with the possessive), thirteenth Lord Commander of the Night’s Watch, who wed a corpse-pale woman beyond the Wall, ruled the Nightfort as a sorcerer-king for thirteen dark years, and was struck from every record — a cautionary tale Old Nan tells, not a monster on the march. The show borrowed the resonance of the name and built a new being under it.",
      "The divergence matters structurally. In the books the Others remain weather — a pressure on the world’s northern edge, undefeatable because unlocated — and whatever answer the novels intend for them is still sealed. The show’s invention made the apocalypse mortal: one being, one heart of ice, one knife. Whether the books’ colder, faceless version will end by sword, pact, or something stranger is among the largest open questions in the saga; the one certainty is that it cannot end the way the show’s did, because the target the show built does not exist on the page.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "He dies at the moment of his victory, in the godswood of Winterfell. The Long Night has gone entirely his way: the Dothraki extinguished, the living penned, his generals fanned out behind him as he walks — unhurried, through dragonfire that does not mark him — to the heart tree where Bran Stark waits as bait that both sides understand is the true prize. Theon Greyjoy’s charge buys seconds; the Night King reaches for his sword to finish the world’s memory in person — and Arya Stark comes out of the dark. He catches her by the throat and the wrist, and she drops the Valyrian dagger from one hand to the other and drives it into him: the same catspaw blade once sent to open Bran’s throat, delivered by the girl no prophecy named. He shatters like river ice, and his lieutenants and his hundred thousand dead go down with him in the same breath — a war eight thousand years in the making, ended in a single movement of a hand. The books, which have no Night King, owe no such death; their long night is still coming.",
    ],
  },
  "Varamyr Sixskins": {
    paras: [
      "Varamyr Sixskins is the greatest skinchanger of the free folk and the tale’s most unsparing study of what the gift does to a soul. Born Lump, a runt in a wildling village, he discovered young — and catastrophically — what was in him: a boy who could slip into a beast’s skin, before he understood what must never be done there. His brother’s death by the family’s dogs, with Lump inside one, got him handed to the warg Haggon, who raised him and taught him the code of the skinchangers: never eat of human meat in a beast’s body, never mate as wolves mate, and never, ever take the skin of another man — the abomination beyond all others. Varamyr repaid his teacher by breaking every law he taught, beginning with tearing Haggon’s own second life from him at his deathbed.",
      "Grown, he made the gift a tyranny. Six beasts wore his will — three wolves, a shadowcat, a snow bear, and the eagle he took from the dead warg Orell — and with them he held a wildling village in fear for years, taking tribute in bread and salt and any woman he pointed at, a small god of a small hell. When Mance Rayder gathered the free folk, Varamyr brought his skins to the host and served as its far-seeing eyes; men stepped wide of him in the camps, and he liked it. The books offer him as the answer to a question the gentler wargs raise: skinchanging is power, and power over beasts is practice, always, for the appetite for power over men.",
      "His prologue to the fifth book is the saga’s deepest dive into warg lore, delivered as a dying man’s inventory. Through Varamyr the reader learns the true rules: the second life, in which a slain skinchanger’s spirit flees into his beast to fade slowly inside it; the taste of the abomination, for he has been inside a man — once, wearing Orell’s eagle-hate — and inside human flesh by way of a wolf’s meal; and the cold mathematics of the gift, that every skin worn leaves some of the wearer behind. He is the lore-keeper the story deliberately makes despicable, so that the knowledge arrives without the comfort of a hero carrying it.",
      "He matters least for what he does and most for what he shows. Bran Stark’s entire arc — the wolf dreams, the temptations, the lines a boy in a beast should not cross — is shadowed by Varamyr’s example: this is what a skinchanger becomes when appetite writes the rules. And his eyes give the books their view of the wildling apocalypse’s aftermath — the shattered host straggling through the snows after the Wall broke it, villages of the starving, the dead beginning to walk. The saga’s most wretched man is one of its most essential windows.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "Varamyr never appears in the show, but his estate is everywhere in it. His warg lore — the second life, the beast that carries a dead man’s grudge — was handed to Orell, whose expanded screen role absorbs both Varamyr’s teaching function and his stolen eagle; his cautionary shadow over Bran’s gift survives in the Three-Eyed Raven’s warnings; and his prologue’s vision of wights rising among the wildling dead was folded into Hardhome. The books also give him a small, crucial on-page role before his prologue: at the battle beneath the Wall it is Varamyr’s beasts and Orell’s inherited eagle that serve Mance as the host’s reconnaissance, and when Melisandre’s fires burn the eagle out of the sky with Varamyr riding it, the agony breaks him — the beginning of the ruin his prologue completes. A book-only figure, and the richest single deposit of skinchanger doctrine in the saga.",
    ],
    fate: [
      "His death opens the fifth book, and it is a masterwork of poetic justice conducted in a snowstorm. Broken by the eagle’s burning and the host’s shattering, Varamyr staggers south among the wildling refugees, robbed, wounded and dying in a nameless village — and at the end, in terror of the true death, he commits the abomination one last time: he hurls his spirit at the spearwife Thistle, the one human being who had shown him kindness, to take her body for his own. She fights him from inside — screaming, biting off her tongue, clawing at her own eyes to force him out — and both of them die in the struggle, her ruined body rising minutes later with blue eyes among the village’s new wights. Varamyr’s spirit escapes into his wolf One Eye for his second life: a fading passenger in a beast in a world filling with the dead, the great skinchanger reduced to exactly what his teacher warned him all men become who love the skin too much. The show, having no Varamyr, never owed him an ending; the books gave him a perfect one.",
    ],
  },
});
